<section class="section reveal">
  <p>Four independent worker processes. Each one has its own event loop, its own copy of your FastAPI app, and its own in-memory state. A request that arrives goes to whichever worker is free.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 05: DOCKER DEPLOYMENT -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num part7-num">05</span>
  <h2>Docker Deployment</h2>
  <p>In practice, you package all of this into a Docker container. The <span class="highlight">multi-stage build</span> is the key pattern — the first stage installs all dependencies, the second stage copies only the installed packages. Your final image stays small and contains nothing it doesn't need.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment"># ── Build stage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────</span>
<span class="code-keyword">FROM</span> python:3.12-slim <span class="code-keyword">AS</span> builder

<span class="code-keyword">WORKDIR</span> /app

<span class="code-comment"># Install dependencies into a separate layer</span>
<span class="code-keyword">COPY</span> requirements.txt .
<span class="code-keyword">RUN</span> pip install --no-cache-dir --target=/app/deps -r requirements.txt

<span class="code-comment"># ── Runtime stage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</span>
<span class="code-keyword">FROM</span> python:3.12-slim

<span class="code-keyword">WORKDIR</span> /app

<span class="code-comment"># Copy installed packages from builder — not pip, not build tools</span>
<span class="code-keyword">COPY</span> --from=builder /app/deps /app/deps
<span class="code-keyword">ENV</span> PYTHONPATH=/app/deps

<span class="code-keyword">COPY</span> . .

<span class="code-keyword">EXPOSE</span> 8000

<span class="code-keyword">CMD</span> ["gunicorn", "main:app",
     "-k", "uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker",
     "-w", "4",
     "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000",
     "--access-logfile", "-",
     "--error-logfile", "-"]</pre>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">To wire Nginx and your FastAPI container together, use Docker Compose. Nginx listens on ports 80 and 443 (public). The <code>api</code> service is only <code>expose</code>d — reachable within the Docker network by Nginx, but not from outside directly. The database is not exposed at all.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment"># docker-compose.yml</span>
<span class="code-keyword">services</span>:
  <span class="code-string">nginx</span>:
    image: nginx:alpine
    ports:
      - <span class="code-string">"80:80"</span>
      - <span class="code-string">"443:443"</span>
    volumes:
      - ./nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
      - ./static:/app/static
      - /etc/letsencrypt:/etc/letsencrypt:ro
    depends_on:
      - api

  <span class="code-string">api</span>:
    build: .
    expose:
      - <span class="code-string">"8000"</span>        <span class="code-comment"># internal only — not ports!</span>
    environment:
      - DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@db:5432/mydb
    depends_on:
      - db

  <span class="code-string">db</span>:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    volumes:
      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    environment:
      - POSTGRES_DB=mydb
      - POSTGRES_USER=user
      - POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pass

<span class="code-keyword">volumes</span>:
  postgres_data:</pre>
  </div>

  <p>This is a complete, production-ready local setup that mirrors what you would run in the cloud.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 06: CLOUD DEPLOYMENT PATTERNS -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num part7-num">06</span>
  <h2>Cloud Deployment Patterns</h2>
  <p>Running <code>docker-compose up</code> on your own server works, but cloud platforms give you managed infrastructure. The stack maps cleanly to each model.</p>
</section>

<div class="cloud-grid reveal">
  <div class="cloud-card cc-aws">
    <span class="cloud-badge">AWS — FULL CONTROL</span>
    <div style="font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.6rem;color:#636e72;margin-bottom:0.5rem">managed infrastructure</div>
    <h4>Route 53 + ALB + ECS/Fargate</h4>
    <p>ALB replaces Nginx for SSL and load balancing. Fargate manages the containers. You provide the Dockerfile — AWS handles the rest.</p>
    <div class="cloud-flow">
Route 53 (DNS)<br>
↓<br>
ALB — SSL, load balancing, health checks<br>
↓<br>
ECS/Fargate — runs your Docker container<br>
↓<br>
RDS (managed Postgres)
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cloud-card cc-simple">
    <span class="cloud-badge">SIMPLE PLATFORMS</span>
    <div style="font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.6rem;color:#636e72;margin-bottom:0.5rem">managed edge layer</div>
    <h4>Railway · Render · Fly.io</h4>
    <p>These platforms handle the Nginx equivalent automatically. Push a Dockerfile and they provide SSL, load balancing, and a public URL. This is the fastest path to production.</p>
    <div class="cloud-flow">
Fly.io / Render / Railway<br>
— handles SSL, DNS, load balancing (their Nginx layer)<br>
↓<br>
Your Docker container<br>
— Gunicorn + Uvicorn + FastAPI
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cloud-card cc-serverless">
    <span class="cloud-badge">SERVERLESS</span>
    <div style="font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.6rem;color:#636e72;margin-bottom:0.5rem">no servers to manage</div>
    <h4>AWS Lambda + Mangum</h4>
    <p>A fundamentally different model. No persistent process, no Gunicorn, no long-lived event loop. <strong>Mangum</strong> is an adapter that translates API Gateway events into ASGI calls.</p>
    <div class="cloud-flow">
API Gateway<br>
↓<br>
Lambda (spins up per request)<br>
— Mangum adapter → ASGI<br>
↓<br>
FastAPI (handles one request, exits)
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p>The serverless tradeoff: no persistent workers means cold starts, no in-memory caching across requests, and a pay-per-invocation cost model. For APIs with unpredictable or very low traffic, it is excellent. For high-throughput, low-latency APIs, the persistent worker model wins.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-keyword">from</span> fastapi <span class="code-keyword">import</span> FastAPI
<span class="code-keyword">from</span> mangum <span class="code-keyword">import</span> Mangum

app = FastAPI()

<span class="code-keyword">@app.get</span>(<span class="code-string">"/hello"</span>)
<span class="code-keyword">async def</span> hello():
    <span class="code-keyword">return</span> {<span class="code-string">"message"</span>: <span class="code-string">"Hello"</span>}

<span class="code-comment"># Lambda handler — Mangum wraps FastAPI</span>
handler = Mangum(app)</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 07: WHAT EACH LAYER OWNS -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num part7-num">07</span>
  <h2>What Each Layer Owns</h2>
  <p>Here is the final, definitive answer to "who does what." No layer crosses into another's territory. Each one does exactly one thing, and does it extremely well.</p>

  <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <table class="layer-own">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="width:22%">Layer</th>
          <th>Responsibility</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="lo-name lo-nginx">Nginx / ALB</span></td>
          <td>SSL/TLS termination, static file serving, load balancing across servers, rate limiting, request buffering, DDoS protection</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="lo-name lo-gunicorn">Gunicorn</span></td>
          <td>Process management, spawning and supervising Uvicorn workers, graceful zero-downtime restarts, crashed worker recovery, pre-fork model</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="lo-name lo-uvicorn">Uvicorn</span></td>
          <td>TCP connections, raw HTTP parsing, ASGI protocol implementation, event loop management, HTTP/1.1 and WebSockets</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="lo-name lo-fastapi">FastAPI</span></td>
          <td>URL routing, request validation (via Pydantic), dependency injection, response serialization, OpenAPI docs generation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="lo-name lo-code">Your Code</span></td>
          <td>Business logic — database queries, calculations, external API calls, the thing your product actually does</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">This is why the stack feels complex at first — it is five separate tools. But once you understand the division, it stops being complex. It becomes obvious.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 08: THE SERIES RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num part7-num">08</span>
  <h2>The Series Recap</h2>
  <p>Seven episodes. The whole picture, assembled.</p>
</section>

<div class="recap-timeline reveal">
  <div class="recap-item">
    <div class="recap-ep">Ep 1</div>
    <div class="recap-body">
      <h4>The request journey and the stack overview</h4>
      <p>We traced a request from browser to function and back. We saw that <code>fastapi dev</code> secretly runs Uvicorn. We learned the four-layer model: Gunicorn → Uvicorn → FastAPI → your code. We introduced ASGI and Starlette.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="recap-item">
    <div class="recap-ep">Ep 2</div>
    <div class="recap-body">
      <h4>Uvicorn: how an HTTP server works in Python</h4>
      <p>We went inside Uvicorn. We saw how raw TCP bytes become structured Python dicts. We learned about <code>uvloop</code> (the C-based event loop) and <code>httptools</code> (the C-based HTTP parser). We understood why "written in Python" does not mean slow.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="recap-item">
    <div class="recap-ep">Ep 3</div>
    <div class="recap-body">
      <h4>The event loop: concurrency without threads</h4>
      <p>We traced the event loop tick by tick. We saw how a single thread handles thousands of simultaneous connections by switching between coroutines at await points. We learned the difference between I/O-bound waiting (where async shines) and CPU-bound work (where it does not).</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="recap-item">
    <div class="recap-ep">Ep 4</div>
    <div class="recap-body">
      <h4>Coroutines: async/await and how FastAPI uses them</h4>
      <p>We unpacked what <code>async def</code> actually creates — a coroutine object, not a running function. We traced <code>await</code> step by step: suspend, yield control, resume. We saw the one silent mistake that kills async performance: blocking code inside an async function without <code>run_in_threadpool</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="recap-item">
    <div class="recap-ep">Ep 5</div>
    <div class="recap-body">
      <h4>Workers: scaling across CPU cores</h4>
      <p>We hit the ceiling of the single event loop — the GIL means one thread per CPU core, one event loop per thread. We saw how Gunicorn's pre-fork model breaks through it: multiple processes, each with its own event loop, each running on its own CPU core.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="recap-item">
    <div class="recap-ep">Ep 6</div>
    <div class="recap-body">
      <h4>FastAPI's magic: routing, validation, docs</h4>
      <p>We opened the FastAPI layer itself. We traced how <code>@app.get("/users/{id}")</code> registers a route in Starlette's router, how Pydantic converts type hints into runtime validators, how dependency injection builds and reuses dependencies per request, and how OpenAPI schema generates <code>/docs</code> automatically.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="recap-item">
    <div class="recap-ep" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#6c5ce7,#0984e3)">Ep 7</div>
    <div class="recap-body">
      <h4>The full production stack</h4>
      <p>This one. The complete picture from internet to function: Nginx at the front door, Gunicorn managing the fleet, Uvicorn handling HTTP and the event loop, FastAPI routing and validating, your code doing the work.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p>If you started this series thinking FastAPI was a magic command that made HTTP work, you now know there is no magic. There is a carefully designed stack of tools, each one solving a specific problem the others cannot.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:1rem">Nginx handles the internet's edge. Gunicorn handles processes. Uvicorn handles async I/O. FastAPI handles application logic. Your code handles your product.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:1rem">That is not complexity for its own sake. That is engineering. Every layer you understand is a problem you can debug, tune, and reason about when something goes wrong at 2am.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem;font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:800;color:#6c5ce7">Now you know the full stack.</p>
</section>

<!-- QUIZ: PROBE YOUR THINKING -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep7">
  <span class="section-num part7-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <!-- Q1: Multiple Choice -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">A request arrives from the internet. Put these layers in the correct order from outermost to innermost:</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Gunicorn → Nginx → Uvicorn → FastAPI → Your Code</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Nginx → Gunicorn → Uvicorn → FastAPI → Your Code</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Nginx → Uvicorn → Gunicorn → FastAPI → Your Code</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Nginx → FastAPI → Gunicorn → Uvicorn → Your Code</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) Nginx → Gunicorn → Uvicorn → FastAPI → Your Code</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Each layer has exactly one job: Nginx handles the internet edge (SSL, static files, rate limiting), Gunicorn manages worker processes, Uvicorn runs the event loop and parses HTTP, FastAPI routes and validates, and your code runs the business logic.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q2: Multiple Choice -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Why does Nginx sit in front of Gunicorn instead of letting clients connect to Gunicorn directly?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Gunicorn is too slow to handle HTTP</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Nginx handles SSL termination, static files, rate limiting, and DDoS protection — things Gunicorn doesn't do</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Gunicorn can't bind to port 80 or 443</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>It's just convention — Gunicorn works fine on its own</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) Nginx handles SSL termination, static files, rate limiting, and DDoS protection — things Gunicorn doesn't do</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Gunicorn is a process manager for Python apps, not a hardened internet-facing server. Nginx is built in C to handle tens of thousands of concurrent connections and absorb malicious traffic before it reaches Python.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q3: True or False -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Docker replaces Nginx in a production deployment.</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>True</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>False</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) False</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Docker is a packaging and isolation tool, not a server. It wraps your entire stack (Nginx + Gunicorn + Uvicorn + FastAPI) in a container. Nginx still runs inside (or beside) the container, doing the same job it always does. Docker solves deployment consistency, not networking.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q4: Multiple Choice -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 04</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">A Uvicorn worker process crashes due to a bug. What happens next?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>The entire application goes down</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Gunicorn detects the crash and automatically spawns a replacement worker</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Nginx restarts the worker</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>The operating system restarts it via systemd</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) Gunicorn detects the crash and automatically spawns a replacement worker</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">This is Gunicorn's core job as a process supervisor — it monitors worker health, restarts crashed workers, and can perform graceful zero-downtime deploys. The other workers continue serving requests while the replacement starts up.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

